>
  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>Besides neurons, the brain contains <strong>glial cells</strong> — supporting tissue. A glioma grows from them. It is the most common primary tumour arising from the brain tissue itself.</p>
    <p>Gliomas are graded by malignancy from 1 to 4:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Grades 1–2</strong> — grow slowly, over years. They are sometimes called low-grade gliomas.</li>
      <li><strong>Grades 3–4</strong> — grow fast. The most aggressive is <strong>glioblastoma</strong>, grade 4.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Today the type and grade of a glioma are determined not only under the microscope but also by molecular features of the tumour. They determine the prognosis and the choice of treatment.</p>
    <p>The key feature of a glioma is that it has no clear capsule. Tumour cells spread into the surrounding brain beyond the border seen on MRI.</p>
    <Scheme />
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Seizures</strong> — a common first sign, especially with slow gliomas.</li>
      <li><strong>Headache</strong>, worse in the morning, with nausea.</li>
      <li><strong>Increasing weakness</strong> or numbness of an arm or leg, problems with speech or vision — depending on where the tumour is.</li>
      <li><strong>Changes in memory and behaviour</strong>, first noticed by family.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Slow gliomas may cause only occasional seizures for years. Aggressive ones lead to noticeable problems within weeks.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Brain MRI with contrast</strong> — the main test: shows the size and location of the tumour and the swelling around it.</li>
      <li><strong>Advanced MRI sequences</strong> — perfusion, spectroscopy, tractography. They help assess tumour aggressiveness and plan surgery so as not to damage important pathways.</li>
      <li><strong>Histology and molecular testing</strong> — the final diagnosis. Done on tissue obtained at surgery or biopsy.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>An MRI report is not yet a diagnosis. Images can suggest a glioma, but only tissue can confirm it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <h3>Surgery</h3>
    <p>The first and main step is <strong>maximal safe resection</strong> of the tumour. The goal is to remove as much as possible while preserving speech, movement and other functions.</p>
    <p>Here <strong>neuronavigation</strong> helps me: the MRI is matched to the patient's head in real time, and I see the tumour borders and important brain areas on the screen. This makes the approach smaller and the removal more precise. An example is <a href={`${tg}/96`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">removal of a large temporal lobe tumour</a> and <a href={`${tg}/97`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">the surgery video</a> (surgical footage, in Russian).</p>
    <p>If the tumour is deep or in an area where removal is too risky, a <strong>biopsy</strong> is done — a piece of tissue is taken for analysis.</p>
    <h3>After surgery</h3>
    <p>Further treatment depends on histology and molecular testing:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>with slow gliomas, after complete removal observation with follow-up MRI is sometimes enough;</li>
      <li>with high-grade gliomas, <strong>radiotherapy and chemotherapy</strong> are usually needed. They are prescribed by an oncologist as decided by the tumour board.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="posle">
    <h2>After surgery</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Follow-up MRI — soon after surgery to assess the extent of removal, then on the oncologist's schedule.</li>
      <li>If there were seizures, antiepileptic drugs continue; the decision to stop them is made by a neurologist.</li>
      <li>With neurological deficits — rehabilitation: exercise therapy, speech therapy.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

</Article>
